  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nMonsoonal flow continues to bring shower and thunderstorm chances\nfor the Silver State through Wednesday, but changes next week \nlook to deliver drier conditions back to the Great Basin. Models \ncontinue to show 20% to 50% chance of isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms, but these chances drop to 10% to 30% \nTuesday and Wednesday, as High pressure over the southern plains \nbuilds west into Arizona. Storm modes through Monday still favor \nmore wet storm modes, but there will still be a chance for a few \ndry thunderstorms, as storm motion increases. Tuesday and \nWednesday as moisture flow becomes restricted the chances for dry \nthunder will increase as well. Common hazards for storms will \ninclude gusty outflow winds up to 50 MPH, ground lightning \nstrikes, and brief heavy rainfall. After Wednesday, models still \nshow that the upper ridge will try and work its way back west and \nshifting the upper flow back to a WSW direction, thus cutting off \nmoisture. What is interesting is models are now split on what will\nbuild in. The ECMWF wants to bring the ridge back over the \nColorado River valley which would mean SW flow and warmer \ntemperatures due to thicker heights. The GFS is wanting to also \nbring the ridge west, but it keep the ridge farther south, and \nwants to bring the Pacific NW under a longwave troughing setup. \nThis will bring more westerly flow, which would be dry, but it \nwould also be cooler given the lower forecast heights by 30 dM \nwhen compared to the EC. So overall confidence remains mixed for \nthe extended, as conditions look dry, but as for winds and \ntemperatures it is uncertain. For now will keep Temperatures in \nthe low 80s to low 90s, overnight lows range into the mid 50s to \nmid 60s. Winds will be breezy through the weekend and into the new\nweek as winds shift more south to southwest in line with the \nupper level flow pattern at speeds of 10 to 20 MPH with gusts up \nto 30 MPH possible. Winds look to shift back to the west after \nWednesday with higher speeds possible through the following \nweekend. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence a daily 20% to 50% chance for isolated \nto scattered showers and thunderstorms for Nevada through Monday. \n\nThere is moderate confidence in a 10% to 30% chance for a mix of \nisolated dry and wet thunderstorms Tuesday and Wednesday.\n\nThere is moderate confidence in dry conditions returning to the \nGreat Basin Thursday trough Sunday of next week.\n\nThere is low confidence in the upper pattern after Wednesday as \nmodels are split on potential solutions for winds, and \ntemperatures.",
